let __v_313 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rabbit\",\"rabbits\",\"bunny\",\"bunnies\"]},\"Yes, especially for a doe: by four or five an unneutered female rabbit has uterine cancer in the great majority of cases, and neutering takes that away. A buck is done for the spraying and the mounting, and two rabbits get on far better once both are done.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cat\",\"cats\",\"kitten\",\"kittens\",\"tom\",\"tomcat\",\"queen\"]},\"Yes: a spayed cat is out of the annual litters (a cat can have three a year), stops the calling that keeps the house awake, and a queen spayed before her first season has a much lower risk of mammary tumours. A tom stops spraying and fighting once he is done. The age is the only real question, and for a cat it is young - about four months.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dog\",\"dogs\",\"puppy\",\"puppies\",\"bitch\",\"bitches\"]},\"Usually yes. For a bitch, spaying before her first season cuts the risk of mammary tumours a long way and removes pyometra, which is an emergency operation; for a dog it stops the roaming, marking and fights and removes the risk of testicular cancer. The trade-off is timing: in a large or giant breed, neutering before the growth plates close is linked to joint trouble later, so many vets wait until 12 to 18 months. Ask your vet about the breed and size - the answer is usually yes, at the right age.\"]}]}")?;
