let __v_1385 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"dog\",\"dogs\",\"puppy\",\"puppies\",\"pup\"]},\"A dog lives around 10 to 13 years, and where it lands depends mostly on size: small breeds often reach 14 to 16, while the biggest breeds are usually 8 to 10. Kept lean, walked daily and seen by a vet once a year, a dog sits at the top of its range.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cat\",\"cats\",\"kitten\",\"kittens\"]},\"An indoor cat lives about 12 to 15 years, and many reach 18 or more. A cat that goes outside averages a few years less, mostly because of roads, so a cat flap with a curfew or a secure garden is what buys the extra years.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rabbit\",\"rabbits\",\"bunny\"]},\"A rabbit lives 8 to 12 years, which is longer than most people expect when they buy one for a child. A rabbit on its own in a small hutch usually dies years earlier than a pair with a run and hay all day, so company and space are the two things that matter.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"hamster\",\"hamsters\"]},\"A hamster lives 2 to 3 years, and a Syrian is at the top of that while a dwarf is usually closer to 2. That short life is normal rather than a sign you did something wrong.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"guinea pig\",\"guinea pigs\"]},\"A guinea pig lives 5 to 7 years, and they do better in pairs because they are herd animals. Hay all day and vitamin C in the food are what keep them going that long.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"budgie\",\"budgies\",\"parrot\",\"cockatiel\"]},\"A budgie lives 8 to 12 years, and a cockatiel or a small parrot 15 to 25. Company matters more than anything else: a bird alone in a cage for years usually has a shorter, sadder life than one with a friend and time out of the cage.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"goldfish\",\"fish\"]},\"A goldfish lives 10 to 20 years in a big enough tank or pond with a filter, which is why the fish bowl is the problem: a bowl with no filter is usually a couple of years at best. Two goldfish want a tank of 100 litres or more.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rat\",\"rats\"]},\"A rat lives 2 to 3 years, and they are social, so a pair lives longer and better than one alone. That short life is the hard part of keeping them.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"ferret\",\"ferrets\"]},\"A ferret lives 6 to 10 years, with the males usually a little longer than the females.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"horse\",\"pony\",\"horses\",\"ponies\"]},\"A horse lives 25 to 30 years, and ponies often reach 35. Teeth and feet decide the last third of that, so a yearly check of both is worth more than anything else.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tortoise\",\"tortoises\"]},\"A tortoise outlives most of us: a horsfield's or a Hermann's lives 40 to 70 years and some reach far beyond that, so it is a pet to leave in a will rather than a short commitment.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"chicken\",\"chickens\",\"hen\",\"hens\"]},\"A back-garden hen lives about 5 to 10 years, though the egg-laying years are nearer 3 to 5, and hybrids bred for laying burn out sooner than the old breeds.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"duck\",\"ducks\"]},\"A duck lives 8 to 12 years, and the big meat breeds sit at the bottom of that while a small call duck can reach the top.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"goose\",\"geese\"]},\"A goose lives 15 to 20 years, and they pair for life, so a lone one is a bad idea rather than a short commitment.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"turkey\",\"turkeys\"]},\"A turkey lives 5 to 10 years as a pet: the broad-breasted breeds are bred to grow so fast that few reach old age, while the heritage breeds are the ones that last.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("_PET_LIFESPAN", __v_1385.clone())?;
