let __v_1655 = Value::from("A rabbit is 150 to 300 pounds a year to keep, and the set-up is the real money: a hutch with a run attached, or a shed, is 200 to 400, then hay all day at 20 to 40 a month, pellets, a few greens, neutering 60 to 120 and the annual jabs. Keep two, because a rabbit alone is a stressed rabbit, and they live eight to ten years.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_RABBIT_LINE", __v_1655.clone())?;
let __v_1656 = Value::from("A hamster is 10 to 20 pounds to buy and 5 to 10 a month to keep: bedding, food and a little veg. The cage is the cost - 60 to 120 pounds for a tank at least 100 by 50cm with a deep base to dig in, not the small barred cage in the shop. Save for the vet too: not every practice treats small furries, and a consultation is 30 to 60 pounds.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_HAMSTER_LINE", __v_1656.clone())?;
let __v_1657 = Value::from("A guinea pig is 10 to 25 pounds each and 200 to 400 a year to keep: a cage at least 120 by 60cm, hay all day, pellets with added vitamin C, and greens every day. They need a friend of their own kind, so two is the real minimum, and they live five to seven years.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_GUINEA_LINE", __v_1657.clone())?;
let __v_1658 = Value::from("A budgie is 15 to 40 pounds and about 150 to 250 a year: a wide cage rather than a tall one, at least 60cm across so it can fly between perches, a friend, and pellets with daily veg. The catch is the vet - an avian vet charges more than a small-animal practice, and a budgie should see one at the first sign of fluffing up.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_BUDGIE_LINE", __v_1658.clone())?;
let __v_1659 = Value::from("A horse costs 4,000 to 8,000 pounds a year all in: livery at 150 to 400 a month, hay and feed, shoes every six weeks at 80 to 120, insurance, worming, teeth and the vet. The horse itself is the cheap part. Never buy one without a vetting and a trial, and budget the first year higher still for tack and rugs.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_HORSE_LINE", __v_1659.clone())?;
let __v_1660 = Value::from("Setting up a tank is 150 to 300 pounds - tank, filter, heater, gravel, a test kit and water treatment - and then 10 to 20 a month for food and electricity. Cycle the tank for four to six weeks before any fish go in, and start with a few hardy ones: most first tanks fail because the fish go in on day one.");
frame.store("SMALL_PET_FISH_LINE", __v_1660.clone())?;
