let __v_8834 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"door\",\"A pet that scratches the door wants through, so the scratching is the way it has learned to ask. Do not open it while the paw is on the door: wait for a pause of a few seconds, then open it, and it learns the pause is what works. Hanging a bell on the door and teaching a touch of the nose to it gives a cat a quieter way to ask, and a dog with a shut door between him and you usually wants company more than the room: put his bed on the side of the door you are on, and leave the door open once he is settled.\"],[\"floor\",\"Scratching the floor or the bedding before lying down is normal: dogs and cats turn and scrape to flatten a spot and leave their scent, and it is not a fault. It is worth answering if it is new or constant: give him a soft bed of his own so he has something to turn round in, check the room is not too warm, and if a dog is scraping hard at hard flooring his claws may be catching, so have them clipped. A cat that scrapes at the carpet by the door is marking, and a mat or a vertical post in that spot takes the behaviour.\"],[\"furniture\",\"Put a tall scratching post right beside the corner she uses and make the sofa less appealing with a throw or double-sided tape for a few weeks: cats scratch to mark territory and to shed the outer claw, so the post has to be tall enough to stretch up and steady enough not to wobble. A dog scratching at the sofa or the cushions is usually digging up a nest, so give him a blanket to scrabble at and a walk before the evening, and if it is one spot in particular put something solid in front of it.\"],[\"furniture-dog\",\"A dog scrabbling at the sofa or the cushions is usually digging itself a nest, so give him a blanket or an old duvet of his own to scrabble at and a walk before the evening. If it is one spot he keeps going at, put something solid in front of it for a fortnight and praise him when he uses his own bed instead. Chewing the furniture is a different problem with a different answer, so say if that is what is happening.\"],[\"skin\",\"A pet scratching itself sore is a skin question, not a behaviour one: fleas are the first thing to rule out, then a food or pollen allergy, and a cat over-grooming a bald patch can be mites or stress. Have the vet look before the skin is broken, because an open scratch gets infected. In the meantime a flea treatment from the vet rather than the supermarket, no shampoo unless the vet names one, and a cone if he will not leave a wound alone.\"],[\"\",\"Give them something that is theirs to scratch and put it where they already do it: a tall, steady post for a cat, and a blanket or a bit of matting for a dog. Cats scratch to mark and to shed claw, so a post beside the sofa stops the sofa being the spot, and a dog scraping at the floor or the bedding before lying down is just making a nest. Clipped claws and a walk before the evening take the rest of it, and scratching that has suddenly become constant, or is leaving the skin raw, is the vet's.\"]]}")?;
